// not MFRC's page structure. No misconceptions/Q&A section (dropped
// sitewide). [TIM: ...] marks spots needing real NM numbers/field
// experience instead of guesses.
//
// Per Tim: filled the "why water dominates here" placeholder. NM is
// desert even in its higher-elevation/snowier regions; roughly 90-94%
// of cases TLS sees are water-driven -- stated explicitly as a field
// read, not a tracked statistic, since it isn't one. Added the soaker-
// hose contrast: adding moisture near a foundation is a legitimate
// technique to keep expansive clay hydrated in wetter parts of the
// country, but would be close to catastrophic here. Closed with the
// timing point -- drainage fixed early prevents the problem, fixed
// late only stops it from getting worse.
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';

const title = 'Drainage and Grading Correction in New Mexico | TLS Foundations';
const description =
  'Why drainage correction is often the right first move in New Mexico foundation repair, what it actually involves, and where its limits are.';

