      { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 3, name: 'Drainage and Grading Correction', item: 'https://www.tlsfoundations.com/drainage-correction-deep-dive' },
    ],
  },
];
---

<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  footer="minimal"
  schema={schema}
  backToServices="drainage-correction"
>
  <div class="tls-article" id="top">
    <section class="page-hero">
      <div class="page-hero-inner">
        <span class="hero-eyebrow">Methods · Drainage &amp; Grading</span>
        <h1>Drainage and Grading —<br /><em>Often the Right First Step</em></h1>
        <p class="page-hero-sub">Sometimes the right repair is not a structural repair at all. What water actually does to a New Mexico foundation, and when fixing where it goes is the whole answer.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <div class="article-outer">
      <article class="article-body">
        <p class="article-lede">Of everything in the toolkit, this is the one method that isn't about installing anything under the house. It's about controlling what's happening above it — and in New Mexico, that's usually where the real problem starts.</p>

        <div class="article-section" id="what-it-is">
          <h2>Redirecting Water, <em>Not Installing Anything</em></h2>
          <p class="opening">Drainage and grading correction means redirecting surface water — downspouts, grading slope, irrigation, drainage swales — away from the foundation, so the soil beneath and around it stops taking on moisture it wasn't built to handle.</p>
          <p>It's not a structural repair. Nothing gets installed under the slab or against the footing. The entire premise is that a foundation problem caused by moisture needs its moisture source addressed before — or sometimes instead of — anything structural is done to the foundation itself.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="article-section" id="why-it-matters-here">
          <h2>Why This Matters <em>in New Mexico Specifically</em></h2>
          <p class="opening">The majority of foundation movement in New Mexico is moisture-driven, which makes drainage correction the most important step on a lot of properties — not a minor add-on to a structural repair, but often the actual fix.</p>
          <p>That's the reason drainage correction is described as the <em>correct first intervention</em> rather than just <em>a</em> first intervention: a structural repair like a pier doesn't change what the water is doing. Install piers on a foundation that's still taking on moisture from bad grading, and the structural fix can hold elevation while the underlying moisture problem keeps working on the soil underneath it.</p>
          <div class="callout-block">
            <span class="callout-label">Why water dominates here</span>
            <p>New Mexico is a desert region — even the higher-elevation areas that get more snow and more moisture than the rest of the state are still fundamentally arid by national standards. In TLS's experience — a consistent read across years of jobs, not a formally tracked statistic — water, usually too much of it, is what's actually driving the movement in the vast majority of cases seen in the field — 90 percent or more.</p>
            <p>That's close to the opposite of what some other parts of the country deal with. In wetter regions, deliberately adding moisture near a foundation — a soaker hose to keep expansive clay hydrated and swollen — can be a legitimate technique to keep a home from settling as the soil dries and shrinks. Doing the same thing in New Mexico would be close to catastrophic: the soil here isn't built to handle that kind of added, sustained moisture, and the swelling or instability it triggers works against the structure instead of protecting it.</p>
            <p>That's why gutters, downspouts, and proper grading solve more foundation problems here than almost anything else in the toolkit — but it depends entirely on when those solutions get applied. Fixing drainage before real damage accumulates prevents the problem; fixing it after the fact stops things from getting worse, but it doesn't undo what's already happened.</p>
          </div>
          <details class="deeper">
            <summary class="deeper-toggle">
              <span>Going Deeper — For the Technically Curious</span>
              <span class="deeper-toggle-icon">+</span>
            </summary>
            <div class="deeper-body">
              <p>The applicable code minimum comes from the International Residential Code, Section R401.3: grade must fall at least 6 inches within the first 10 feet surrounding the foundation — a minimum 5% slope. Where a lot line, wall, or existing grade makes that fall impossible, the code allows a drain or swale to do the same job instead of dirt alone.</p>
              <p>Paved surfaces closer to the foundation — a patio, a walkway — carry their own separate minimum: at least a 2% slope away from the building within that same first 10 feet.</p>
              <p class="deeper-cite">Source: International Residential Code (IRC), Section R401.3, Drainage.</p>
            </div>
          </details>
        </div>

        <div class="article-section" id="which-one">
          <h2>Moisture Problem, or <em>Bearing Capacity Problem?</em></h2>
          <p class="opening">Moisture problems and bearing-capacity problems can leave the same marks on a house, which is exactly why treating every crack as "soil that can't hold the house" skips the cheaper, far more common story: water showed up where the design didn't expect it.</p>
          <p>A few real clues point toward moisture as the first suspect: symptoms that showed up after a leak, a grading change, or a wet season; movement concentrated on the side of the house that tracks a downspout or a low spot; movement that's seasonal and partly recovers; the same soil under a dry portion of the house staying quiet. Clues that point toward the soil's actual capacity being the issue instead: soft or undocumented fill under part of the footprint, settlement that continues after moisture sources are already controlled, new load added without a corresponding change in support, or a soil report that already documented weak or collapsible layers at footing depth.</p>
          <p>The distinction isn't academic. If moisture is genuinely driving the movement, piers installed into that same wet, active zone can still see force from the soil around them — the structural fix doesn't neutralize what the water is doing. If the soil at depth is actually competent and the surface is the real problem, the first work is water, not steel.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="article-section" id="limits">
          <h2>Where It <em>Has Real Limits</em></h2>
          <p class="opening">Drainage correction isn't a universal fix, and treating it like one is its own kind of mistake.</p>
          <p>Once soil has already lost its bearing capacity — not just wet, but structurally compromised — correcting the moisture going forward doesn't undo the damage already done. And when the cause is geological rather than surface-water-driven — Mancos Shale heave, karst voids in the Pecos Valley, mine subsidence in the Raton Basin — drainage correction alone won't touch it, because the water isn't coming from where a downspout extension or a regrade can reach.</p>
        </div>

        <a href="#top" class="back-to-top">↑ Back to top</a>

        <p class="closing-link"><a href="/foundation-repair-methods" class="body-link">← Back to Foundation Repair Methods</a><a href="/glossary#drainage-correction" class="body-link">Glossary: Drainage Correction →</a></p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>

